# `.github#1794`'s ELEVEN-LEG MUTATION MATRIX, filed as a spec rather than left in a scratch directory.
#
# WHAT IT ADJUDICATES. `#1794` closed a fail-open in `Reads.openIssues`: an element with no numeric
# `number` was DROPPED SILENTLY, and an absent or ill-typed `body` was read as `""` — which parses to
# `Undeclared`, a confident *"this issue declares nothing"* about a row nobody could read. Since `#1779`
# that read is the `#353` collision gate's candidate set, where a wrong `DISJOINT` is FINAL: there is no
# CAS on a file, so nothing re-decides it and nothing detects it. Each leg below reverts ONE of those
# guards and requires the corpus to say so.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN A SCRATCHPAD — `.github#1825` AC4, decided YES. `#1825` left the question
# open and called it "the more valuable half of the work". A matrix that lives in one worker's scratch
# directory cannot be re-run by the next person to touch `Reads.openIssues`, which is exactly when it is
# worth running; and `#1794`'s own account of it (a table in a merged PR body) is not a thing anybody can
# execute. It is here, and it is a SPEC rather than a script, because the script it would have been
# already landed: `scripts/gate-mutate.py` over `scripts/lib/mutation.py` (`.github#1810`, per `#1808`).
#
#     scripts/gate-mutate.py --specs tests/coord-engine-mutation/specs.yml
#
# ON "IS THIS ABSORBED BY #1810's HARNESS?" — the answer is: the LIBRARY yes, the CORPUS no, and the two
# were worth separating. `#1810`'s `tests/gate-mutation/specs.yml` mutates a GATE's subject and asserts a
# workflow's selftest fires; this mutates ENGINE SOURCE and asserts the unit + parity legs fire. Different
# subjects, different plumbing, and `#1825`'s dispatcher was right that generalising ACROSS that boundary
# is more work than running eleven legs. But the four-point specification, the three-valued verdict, the
# producer-integrity hash and the restore-in-`finally` are exactly the same mechanism, and re-typing them
# here would have been the fifth harness in three days to ship an anchor check with a defect in it. So:
# this corpus, that library.
#
# THE COMMAND IS `tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh` FOR ALL ELEVEN LEGS, and it is the anchor's producer.
# It rebuilds the engine (the mutations edit F# source; the parity corpus drives a COMPILED binary), runs
# both unit projects that carry `#1794` surface, runs the 641-assertion parity corpus, and prints ONE
# tally. It is never a mutation target, so no mutation here can delete the evidence that it ran —
# specification point 3, which `scripts/lib/mutation.py` REFUSES a spec for violating and re-checks by
# hashing the producer either side of every edit.
#
# ANCHORS INSIDE THE PARITY CORPUS ARE A SEPARATE FIX, IN THE SAME PR. `#1794`'s `refute` legs anchored on
# `"could not be read"` — the text the guard itself emits — so M2/M3 deleted the anchor along with the
# guard and the leg read NOT MEASURED exactly where it should have read FAIL. See the block above
# `refute()` in `tests/coord-engine-parity/run.sh`. Both halves had to land together: re-measuring under
# repaired anchors is the only way the verdicts below describe the harness that produced them.

mutations:
  # ===================================================================================================
  # M1-M4 — `Reads.openIssues`: the parse arms. RE-MEASURED here, not quoted from `#1794`.
  # ===================================================================================================
  - id: M1/element-dropped-again
    gate: "#1794 Reads.openIssues: an unidentifiable element refuses the read"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: src/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/Reads.fs
    find: |-
      | true, n when n.ValueKind <> JsonValueKind.Number ->
                                  Error(
                                      Malformed(
                                          subject,
                                          $"element %d{index} of %d{total} has a `number` that is not a number (%A{n.ValueKind}) — an issue that cannot be identified cannot be scanned for a lock, and dropping it would report every claim on it as free"
                                      )
                                  )
                              | false, _ ->
                                  Error(
                                      Malformed(
                                          subject,
                                          $"element %d{index} of %d{total} carries no `number` — an issue that cannot be identified cannot be scanned for a lock, and dropping it would report every claim on it as free"
                                      )
                                  )
    replace: |-
      | true, n when n.ValueKind <> JsonValueKind.Number -> Ok None
                              | false, _ -> Ok None
    breaks: >-
      an element with no numeric `number` is silently discarded again, so the issue vanishes from the
      claim scan, its lock reserves nothing, and every claim on it reads as free — the #1150 fail-open
      one function over, and the one this read shipped with
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  - id: M2/absent-body-reads-empty
    gate: "#1794 Reads.openIssues: an absent `body` is BodyUnread, not an empty declaration"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: src/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/Reads.fs
    find: '| false, _ -> BodyUnread "the element carries no `body` field"'
    replace: '| false, _ -> BodyRead ""'
    breaks: >-
      an element carrying no `body` key reads as an empty body, which `TouchSet.parse` answers
      `Undeclared` and `TouchSet.conflicts` clears against everything — asserting "this issue declares
      nothing" about a row nobody read
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  - id: M3/illtyped-body-reads-empty
    gate: "#1794 Reads.openIssues: an ill-typed `body` is BodyUnread, not an empty declaration"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: src/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/Reads.fs
    find: 'BodyUnread $"the `body` field is a %A{b.ValueKind}, not a string or null"'
    replace: 'BodyRead ""'
    breaks: >-
      a `body` that is an object/number/array reads as an empty body and clears against everything —
      the same false "declares nothing" as M2, reached by a JSON kind change rather than an absence
    note: >-
      THIS IS THE LEG THAT FOUND THE ANCHOR RULE. Under the pre-#1825 harness the parity `refute` legs
      reported NOT MEASURED here rather than FAIL, because their positive anchor was the guard's own
      output. Re-measured under the repaired anchor.
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  - id: M4/null-body-becomes-unreadable
    gate: "#1794 Reads.openIssues: `body: null` stays a SUCCESSFUL read (the over-correction leg)"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: src/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/Reads.fs
    find: '| true, b when b.ValueKind = JsonValueKind.Null -> BodyRead ""'
    replace: '| true, b when b.ValueKind = JsonValueKind.Null -> BodyUnread "M4: the over-correction — a real, observed, empty body read as one nobody could read"'
    breaks: >-
      GitHub's `"body": null` for a description-less issue becomes UNREADABLE rather than empty, which
      would red the scheduler for every issue on the board that nobody wrote a description for
    note: >-
      The one leg guarding against OVER-correction rather than under-correction. #1794 measured it
      KILLED BY EXACTLY ONE CHECK and said so; that thinness is worth re-confirming, not smoothing over.
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  # ===================================================================================================
  # M5-M7 — `Client.activeCollisions`: what the scan DOES with a row it could not read.
  # ===================================================================================================
  - id: M5/unreadable-row-discarded-by-token-filter
    gate: "#1794 activeCollisions: an unreadable row survives the token filter as UNDETERMINED"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli/Client.fs
    find: '| Reads.BodyUnread reason -> Some(other, Choice2Of2 reason)'
    replace: '| Reads.BodyUnread _ -> None'
    breaks: >-
      a row whose body could not be read is dropped by the pure token filter, before any marker is read
      — so it can never be compared and is therefore CLEARED, which is the fail-open, because the only
      thing downstream of this function is a DISJOINT that nothing re-decides
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  - id: M6/held-unreadable-row-skipped-not-refused
    gate: "#1794 activeCollisions: a HELD unreadable row REFUSES rather than being skipped"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli/Client.fs
    find: |-
      | Choice2Of2 reason ->
                                          Error(
                                              Errors.Malformed(
                                                  $"%s{ref.Short}'s collision scan",
                                                  $"%s{other.Short} is held by %s{m.Worker.Value} and its touch-set could not be read (%s{reason}) — refusing to report DISJOINT over a live reservation whose surface is unknown (#1794/#266)"
                                              )
                                          )
    replace: '| Choice2Of2 _ -> scan acc rest'
    breaks: >-
      a row that IS held, whose touch-set could not be read, is skipped instead of refusing — so the
      scan answers DISJOINT over a live reservation whose surface nobody saw. #266's "I could not look"
      turned back into "I looked and it was fine", on the one arm where the answer is final
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  - id: M7/refuses-on-any-unreadable-row
    gate: "#1794 activeCollisions: an UNHELD unreadable row does NOT red the scan (the over-correction leg)"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli/Client.fs
    find: |-
      match Reads.reserver opts.LeaseMinutes markers with
                              | None -> scan acc rest
    replace: |-
      match Reads.reserver opts.LeaseMinutes markers with
                              | None when (match verdict with
                                           | Choice2Of2 _ -> false
                                           | _ -> true) -> scan acc rest
                              | None ->
                                  Error(
                                      Errors.Malformed(
                                          $"%s{ref.Short}'s collision scan",
                                          $"%s{other.Short} has a touch-set that could not be read — M7: refusing over a row NOBODY holds"
                                      )
                                  )
    breaks: >-
      the scan refuses on ANY unreadable row, held or not — so one anomaly on an issue nobody is
      standing in reds `widen`/`overlap`/`set-paths` for the whole repo. Fail-closed became
      fail-loud-everywhere, which is the failure that gets a correct guard reverted
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  # ===================================================================================================
  # M8-M9 — the two routes that reach the same read from somewhere else.
  # ===================================================================================================
  - id: M8/offboard-sweep-reserves-nothing
    gate: "#1794 Scan.snapshot's off-board sweep: an unreadable body is RvUnreadable, not nothing"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: src/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/Scan.fs
    find: '| Reads.BodyUnread reason -> Some(RvUnreadable reason)'
    replace: '| Reads.BodyUnread _ -> None'
    breaks: >-
      the off-board sweep lets an unreadable body reserve NOTHING, so a live claim on a row nobody could
      read stops reserving and a candidate overlapping its real files is handed the tree its holder is
      standing in — #1150's own fail-open, on the arm #1150 did not reach
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  - id: M9/pagination-disabled-in-the-engine
    gate: "#1794 Transport.Send: `openIssues` is PAGINATED, and the promise is kept"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: src/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/Transport.fs
    find: 'follow first first.NextLink 100'
    replace: 'follow first None 100'
    breaks: >-
      the transport stops following `Link: rel=next`, so every collection truncates at page one and the
      claim scan reports every lock past it as FREE
    note: >-
      THE ONLY LEG THAT MUTATES THE ENGINE RATHER THAN THE FIXTURE to disable pagination. `OIP_NO_LINK=1`
      is the fixture-side negative control and it lives in the parity corpus; this is the stronger form,
      because a fixture that withholds a header cannot show that the ENGINE would have followed one.
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  # ===================================================================================================
  # M10 — THE LEG THAT MUTATES THE FIXTURE. Specification point 4 (`#1808`), and it is not optional:
  # without it `refute` is an untested guard guarding against untested guards.
  # ===================================================================================================
  - id: M10/fixture-cannot-bind
    gate: "#1794/#1815 refute(): a leg whose command never ran reports NOT MEASURED, never PASS"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: tests/coord-engine-parity/openissuespage_server.py
    find: 'srv = ThreadingHTTPServer(("127.0.0.1", 0), H)'
    replace: 'srv = ThreadingHTTPServer(("203.0.113.1", 0), H)'
    breaks: >-
      the fixture binds an address that is not local (TEST-NET-3, RFC 5737) and therefore cannot bind at
      all, so it prints no port, the engine is never invoked, and every `#1794` refutation is made over
      an output that does not exist
    note: >-
      THE VERDICT TO READ CAREFULLY. This leg does not assert that the ENGINE noticed anything — the
      engine never runs. It asserts that the HARNESS did: `refute` must report NOT MEASURED (and red the
      suite) rather than observe that the forbidden string is absent and call that a pass. The first
      draft of these legs had exactly that defect five times over, and no assertion about the happy path
      can see it, because the happy path is where the fixture DOES run.
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh

  # ===================================================================================================
  # M11 — #1849's migrated corpus, proved on one of the 26 formerly raw absence conclusions.
  # ===================================================================================================
  - id: M11/migrated-refute-has-no-measurement
    gate: "#1849 refute(): a migrated raw absence leg reports NOT MEASURED when its output disappears"
    command: ["bash", "tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh"]
    target: tests/coord-engine-parity/run.sh
    find: 'l73="$(printf ''%s'' "$err" | grep ''FS.GG.SDD#73'')"'
    replace: 'l73="" # M11: erase the command output that anchors the migrated #428 refutation'
    breaks: >-
      the migrated #428 negative leg receives no batch-member verdict at all. Its forbidden holder text
      is therefore absent, exactly as it was in the old raw `*) ok` arm, but the new positive anchor
      cannot match and the leg must report NOT-MEASURED instead of PASS
    note: >-
      This mutates the parity fixture rather than engine behavior deliberately: the acceptance property
      is the harness's third verdict when a command produces no evidence. The mutation leg preserves
      the parity log's `NOT-MEASURED #428` line and counts it as a fired assertion in its terminal tally.
    anchor:
      pattern: 'coord-engine-mutation leg: \d+ passed, \d+ failed'
      produced_by: tests/coord-engine-mutation/leg.sh
